# browser-qa-refresh@.timer (OPEN-125 / Issue #87)
#
# TEMPLATE unit, instantiated per persona as browser-qa-refresh@<persona>.timer.
# Cadence ratified in the OPEN-125 design freeze (D7) -- keeps sessions fresh without
# depending on a measured session_expiry value. Reevaluate only as a separate, explicit,
# controlled change after real measurement, never silently.
#
# ROOT CAUSE (confirmed 2026-09-12, regression discovered same day, see Issue #87):
# the original cadence used ONLY monotonic specs (OnBootSec + OnUnitActiveSec, no
# OnCalendar). OnUnitActiveSec is computed from the TRIGGERED SERVICE UNIT's own
# in-memory ActiveEnterTimestamp -- a value that lives purely in the systemd manager's
# runtime state, is NEVER persisted to disk, and is silently wiped whenever that
# service unit's runtime object is recreated (observed: a `systemctl daemon-reload`
# following a redeploy of these unit files, done shortly after the very first timer
# fire on 2026-09-04, before the first 6h re-arm could happen). Once wiped, with
# OnBootSec already consumed for the current boot, OnUnitActiveSec has no anchor left
# to schedule from -- the timer stays "active (elapsed)" forever with no NEXT run,
# until either a reboot (re-arms OnBootSec) or a manual `systemctl start` of the
# .service. `Persistent=true` does NOT help here: it only affects catch-up for
# OnCalendar=, never for monotonic specs (see systemd.timer(5)). Confirmed via
# `systemctl show browser-qa-refresh@<persona>.service -p ActiveEnterTimestamp` (blank)
# vs. the TIMER's own `LastTriggerUSec` (populated, and older than the unit files'
# on-disk mtime) -- i.e. the service's own activation memory was lost after it had
# already fired once.
#
# FIX: OnCalendar= is evaluated fresh from wall-clock time on every check -- it has no
# dependency on the triggered unit's volatile activation memory, so it survives any
# future daemon-reload/redeploy. Persistent=true now does real work: if the host is
# down (or the timer briefly inactive) across a scheduled boundary, the missed run is
# caught up via systemd's on-disk calendar timer stamp
# (/var/lib/systemd/timers/stamp-<unit>.timer), which OnCalendar= timers -- unlike pure
# monotonic ones -- actually maintain. OnBootSec=5min is kept for a fast first refresh
# right after an actual reboot; it is harmless alongside OnCalendar=.

[Unit]
Description=Periodic browser QA storageState refresh for persona %i (OPEN-125)
Documentation=https://github.com/Tweezer1/arkonex-ops-docs/issues/87

[Timer]
OnBootSec=5min
OnCalendar=*-*-* 00/6:00:00
Persistent=true
RandomizedDelaySec=5min

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
